opr -- Command

Send a print job to the spooler for a named printer
opr [-lp | -vp] [lpr options] [file ...]

opr chooses a spooler and becomes it.  It looks at its first argument, and if
that argument names one of the printers it knows about, it removes it from the
command line and execs the program that serves that printer; everything else
on the command line, options and file names alike, is passed through
unchanged.

    -lp		/bin/lpr, the line printer.  This is the default.
    -vp		/bin/vpr, the versatec plotter.

With no printer argument, or with a first argument that is not one of the two
above, opr execs /bin/lpr.  So `opr -c3 file' is `lpr -c3 file', and
`opr -vp -c3 file' is `vpr -c3 file'.

Since opr replaces itself with the spooler, its options, its behaviour and its
exit status are the spooler's from that point on.  It has no options of its
own beyond the printer names, reads no files itself and spools nothing.

Diagnostics
    opr: -vp unavailable

The spooler for that printer could not be executed -- on this system, because
/bin/vpr is not installed; there is no versatec plotter on a Commodore 900 and
nothing builds one.  Exit status is 1.

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it.  It was written here from
the source in base/cmd/lpr; none of Mark Williams Company's text is in it.

The list of printers is compiled in, so a site with a second printer changes
opr rather than a configuration file.  With only /bin/lpr installed here, opr
is a name for lpr and nothing more.

